== The Ninth Sermon

#quote[And it will be said in that day, “Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him, and He will save us. This is the #smallcaps[Lord]; we have waited for Him; we will be glad and rejoice in His salvation.”—Isa 25:9.]

In the worst age of the church, so that the church may not be swallowed up with fear, in the worst times, God prepares promises for his people. It was the case of our blessed Savior himself with his poor disciples, that they might not be overwhelmed with sorrow. Therefore he adds sacraments to the Passover, and the New Testament to the Old, and all to confirm faith, knowing that our hearts are very apt to be daunted.

The Lord promises here a feast of fat things, and all things belonging to a feast, the best of the best, and the removal of all that may hinder joy, such as taking away the veil, which hinders them from the sight of it. And then death is swallowed up in victory, as it already is in our Head, who is gloriously triumphing in heaven; and then all tears will be wiped from all faces. There is a turn and change of things. They are now in a valley of tears, but it will not always be so. The time will come when all tears will be wiped away, and the cause of all tears, which is sorrow. The rebukes of his people will be taken away; the scandal that lies upon the best things will be taken away. The worst things go under a better appearance, and the best things under a veil; but one day, as things are, so they will be seen. The God of truth will have truth made clear enough. And all this is sealed up with the highest authority, which admits of no contradiction: “The #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts has spoken it.”

We came last time to these words, “Behold, this is our God.” In them we may consider, first of all, that God has left to his church rich and precious promises, such as are spoken of before: a feast, and the removal of all hindrances whatever. He not only grants heaven when we die, and eternal happiness; but in this world, on our way, he grants precious promises to support our faith, so that we may begin heaven upon earth. What these promises are we showed last time.

The second observation was that God’s people are here in a state of expectation. It will be said, “Behold, we have waited for Him.” We are in a condition of waiting while we live in this world, because we are not at home. Our state requires waiting; heaven requires settledness and rest. There all appetites, all desires will be satisfied to the full. Our state here is a passage to a better state, and waiting is a disposition fit for such a condition.

And in this there is both good and imperfection. Good, that we have something to wait for; imperfection, that we have to wait for it, that we do not have it in possession; and until we are in heaven we are in a state of waiting. In Revelation, “Come, Lord Jesus, come quickly” (Rev 22:20), a glorious state of a church is set forth; but until all is done, it does not have what it would have. We cannot be in such a state in the world; but there is room for a desire, namely, for immediate and eternal communion with Christ in heaven. And therefore “it will be said in that day, ‘Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him.’”

I will add a little on this state of waiting before I go further. God will not have our condition perfect at once, but will have us continue in a state of waiting.

Reason 1. First of all, it is his pleasure that we should live by faith, and not by sight. We have sense and feeling of many things; he does not reserve everything for heaven. How many sweet refreshments we have on the way! But the course of our life is by faith, and not by sight. God will have us in such a condition.

Reason 2. Again, we are not fitted here for the sight of the glory to come. Our vessels cannot hold that glory. A few drops of that happiness so overcame Peter in the transfiguration that he did not know himself.

Reason 3. God is so good to us that he would have us enjoy the best at the last. Heaven is the sweeter, the harder our way there is. Heaven is heaven, and happiness is happiness, after a long time of waiting. For waiting enlarges the capacity and desires of the soul to receive more; it sets off the happiness afterward. And therefore God keeps the best for the last, because he will never interrupt the happiness of his children. When they are in heaven, every cause of sorrow is banished. He will have a distinction between the church militant and the church triumphant. He will train up his children here before he brings them to heaven. He will perfume his spouse, and make her fit for an everlasting communion with him in heaven. The third thing is that, as there are promises, and these promises are not fulfilled at once, which puts us in a state of waiting, so God gives grace to uphold us in waiting. Waiting is not an empty time, to wait so long and have no grace in the meantime; but waiting is a time of fitting us for what we are to receive afterward.

We see in nature that winter is a dull time compared with the spring and harvest, and the weather is very cold; yet it ripens and mellows the soil, and fits it for the spring. A great deal is done toward the harvest in winter. It is not a sheer distance of time. So between the promise and heaven itself, it is not merely a waiting time, and there is an end of it; but it is a time taken up by the Spirit of God in preparing the heart, in subduing all base lusts, and in taking us off from ourselves and from whatever is contrary to heaven. The time is filled up with a great deal of what fits us for glory in heaven. The gracious God who fits us for heaven, and heaven for us, furnishes us with all the graces necessary for that condition: faith to believe; patience to wait for, and to depend on, what one does not see, to be above sense; a grace of hope to wait for what one believes, to be an anchor to the soul in all conditions whatever. And then a grace of patience to wait meekly all the while. And then long-suffering, patience lengthened out. As the tedious stretch between us and heaven is long, so there are lengthening graces. We would have everything at once: “How long, Lord, how long?” (Rev 6:10). We are so impatient, even David and others; and therefore God gives grace to hold out and lengthen our spiritual faith, and hope, and perseverance, and constant courage to meet all difficulties on the way. When the spirit of a man beholds heaven, and happiness, and God, it makes him constant, in some sort like the things he beholds, for the spirit transforms him into the likeness of the object. Now, he beholds a constant covenant; and as faith looks upon a constant God, constant happiness, and constant promises, it shapes the soul to suit the excellence of the object it lays hold on.

And then the Spirit of God, on the way to heaven, subdues all evil murmurings and objections, not allowing us to put out our hands to any iniquity. Though we do not have what we would have, he keeps us in a good and fruitful way; for to wait is not only to endure, but to endure in a good course, fitting us for happiness, until grace ends in glory.

In the fourth place, God will perform all his promises in time. As the church says here, “This is the #smallcaps[Lord]; we have waited for Him.” Now he has made good whatever he has said.

To enlarge on this point a little. As there is a time of waiting, so there will be a time when God’s people will say, “Behold, this is the #smallcaps[Lord]; we have waited for Him.” Why?

Reason 1. God is Jehovah. A full and pregnant word! A word of comfort and support for the soul, this word Jehovah! He is a God who gives being to all things, and being to his word, and therefore what he says he will make good. He is Lord of his word. Every man’s word is as his nature, and power, and ability are: the word of a man, or the word of an honest man; but this being the word of a God, he will make all good.

Reason 2. And then he will make all good, because he is faithful. God says it, and he will do it.

Reason 3. You#super[+] need no more reason than his pity for his people, his deepest compassion. The hearts of people would fail if he should delay too long. And therefore out of his tender mercy, in his time, which is the best time, not only because he is faithful, but because he is loving and full of pity, he will make good all his promises. And then he will do it.

Reason 4. For what is grace, but a down payment of the fullness we will have in heaven? What is peace here but a down payment of that peace in heaven? And what is joy here but a down payment of fullness of joy forevermore? And will God lose his down payment? Therefore we will enjoy what God has promised, and what we expect, because we have the down payment. It is not a pledge only, for a pledge may be taken back, but a down payment, which is never taken back, but is made up into the full bargain. Grace is made up in glory, as beginnings are made up with perfection. Where God lays a foundation, he will perfect it. Where God gives the firstfruits, he will give the harvest.

But it will be a long time first, because he will exercise every grace to the uttermost. You#super[+] see how Abraham was brought to the very last. On the mountain God provides for a sacrifice, when the knife was ready to seize on Isaac’s throat, Gen 22:12-14.

We should answer God’s dealing with our faith; that is, if God delays, let us wait, indeed, wait to the uttermost, wait to death. He is our God to death, and in death, and forever. If God performs his promise at the worst, then, until we are at the lowest, we must wait.

And, therefore, one mark that sets a child of God apart from others is this. “Give me the present,” says the carnal, brutish man, the world; but God’s people are content to wait. The child of God knows that what he has in promise is better than what he has in possession. The gleanings of God’s people are better than the harvest of the others. The others cannot wait, but must have present payment. God’s child can wait, for he lives by faith. And therefore we should learn patiently to wait for the performance of all God’s promises.

And to give a little direction in that, remember some rules, which every man may gather for himself, such as,

+ God’s time is the best time. Deus est optimus arbiter opportunitatis, the best discerner of opportunities. And “on the mountain God will be seen.” Though he delays long, he will come, and not delay too long; and then all the strength of the enemy is with God. Robur hostium apud Deum. The strength of the enemy is in his hand; he can suspend it when he pleases.
+ Then, though God seems to carry things by ways contrary to what he promises, which makes waiting so difficult, yet he will bring things about at last. He promises happiness, and there is nothing but misery. He promises forgiveness, and opens the conscience to cry out against sin. Yes, but Luther’s rule is exceedingly good in this case. Summa ars, the greatest art of a Christian is credere credibilia, and sperare dilata, to hope for things a long time, and to believe God when he seems contrary to himself in his promise.

But though God delays, yet in that day he performs. It is set down without a fixed date, for it is not fit that we should know the particular time. And therefore he says, “in that day.” He does not set down a particular time, but “in that day,” in which he means to be glorious in the performance of his promise. There is a time, and a set time, and a short time too, in regard to God, and a fit time. If the time were shorter than God has appointed, it would be too short; if longer, too long. “My times,” says David, “are in Your hands” (Ps 31:15). If they were in the enemy’s hands, we would never be out of trouble; if in our own, we would never enter it; if in our friends’ hands, their goodwill would be more than their ability. “But my times”—he does not say, “my time,” but—“my times are in Your hands,” that is, my times of trouble and times of waiting. And it is well that they are in God’s hands, for he has a day, and a certain day, and a fit day to answer the waiting of all his people.

And when that day has come, you#super[+] see how their hearts are enlarged; they will say, “This is the #smallcaps[Lord]; we have waited for Him.”

When God means to perform his promise, either in this world or in the world to come, especially in the world to come, when there will be the completion of all promises, God will enlarge the hearts of his people. “This is the #smallcaps[Lord]; we have waited for Him.” “This is the #smallcaps[Lord].” He repeats it again and again.

Our soul has a great capacity, being a spiritual substance; and then God will fill the soul, and make it take in misery, or take in happiness, when every corner of the soul will be filled; and then, since men have bodies too, it is fit that the bodies should have a part; so the whole man will show forth the justice or the mercy of God.

From the nature of the thing, it cannot be otherwise. Every member of the body will be fit to glorify God. What the psalmist says of his tongue, “Awake, my glory,” he may say of every member: Do your office in glorifying the Lord and rejoicing in the Lord. Pectus facit disertos. The heart makes a man eloquent and full. So the performance of any promise fills the heart so full of affections, the affections are so enlarged; and therefore we must not be fond of a courtly kind of expression, as they were in old times, and the like court eloquence, when men might not speak fully. But when joy possesses the heart to the full, there are full expressions: “This is the #smallcaps[Lord], this is the #smallcaps[Lord]; let us rejoice in Him.” And therefore there seem to be so many tautologies, so many repetitions, in the Psalms, though they are no tautologies, but sheer overflowings of a sanctified affection.

Oh! beloved, what a blessed time that will be, when this large heart of ours will have what will fill it; when the best parts of us, our understanding, will, and affections, will be carried to what is better and larger than themselves, and will be, as it were, swallowed up in the fullness of God. And that is the reason for the repetition of the word, “This is the #smallcaps[Lord], this is the #smallcaps[Lord].”

And it follows, “We will rejoice and be glad in His salvation.” When a gracious heart is full of joy, how does it express that joy? A wicked heart, when it is full of joy, is like a dirty river that runs over its banks and carries a great deal of filth with it: dirty expressions. But when a gracious heart expresses itself, being full of joy, it expresses itself in thanks and praises, in stirring up others. “Behold, this is our God; we will rejoice and be glad in His salvation.” “Is anyone cheerful?” says the apostle St. James, “He should sing praises” (Jas 5:13). God has affections for any condition. “Is a man in misery? Let him pray.” This is a time of mourning. Does God perform any promise, and so give cause for joy? Let him sing. There is an action for every affection, an affection for every condition. And this may stir us up to begin the employment of heaven here on earth. We will say so in heaven, “Behold, this is the #smallcaps[Lord]; we have waited for Him.”

For every performance of promises, be much in thankfulness. “Our way of life is in heaven,” says the apostle, Phil 3:20. And what is the greatest part of a Christian’s way of life, but to give thanks in all things? Here the holy church says so because their matter of praise was too big for their soul, and therefore they broke out in this manner. And so it often is with a child of God. His heart is so full that it is too big for his body in expressing the matter of praise. But it is his comfort that in heaven he will have a large heart, equal to the large occasion of praise. I will not enlarge on the common topic of thanksgiving.

In this condition we can never be miserable; for it springs from joy, and joy disposes a man to thankfulness, and upon thankfulness follows peace; and can we be miserable in peace of conscience? Therefore, says the apostle, “In all things give thanks, and let your#super[+] requests be made known to God” (Phil 4:6). And what will follow on that, when I have made known my requests, and paid my tribute of thanks? “Then the peace of God, which passes understanding, will guide your#super[+] mind” (Phil 4:7). When we have paid God the tribute we can pay him, then the soul, as one that has discharged a debt, is at peace. I have prayed to God, I have laid my petition in his bosom, I am not in arrears for former favors; “therefore the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your#super[+] hearts and minds.” Hannah had prayed once, did not go away, but prayed again, 1 Sam 2:1. The happiness of heaven follows the actions of heaven. Praising being the main employment of heaven, the happiness and comfort of heaven follow.

And however these promises are fulfilled in heaven, yet they have a gradual performance on earth. For he speaks certainly of the state of the Jews yet to come, in which there will be the fulfilling of all these promises.

“We have waited for Him; He will save us.” Experience of God’s performance always stirs them up to wait for him, and to rejoice in his salvation. Experience stirs up hope. The beginning of a Christian, and the middle, is to hope for the end; and surely our beginning should help the latter end! All a Christian’s life should help the end. All former things should come in and help his latter.

Beloved, we are too backward in this, in treasuring up the benefit of experience. There are few people of any years who could not tell stories of God’s gracious dealings with them, if all were kept: the comforts past, and those for the time to come, and all little enough. It was David’s course: “You are my God from my mother’s womb, and on You I have leaned ever since I was born; do not fail me when I am old” (Ps 22:10). Go along with God’s favors, and use them as arguments for future blessings. As former victories help to win the second victory, every former favor helps to strengthen our faith.

Next, God is a fountain never drained, and when we have to deal with an infinite God, the more we take of him the more we offer him. It is no good plea to say, You have done kindnesses, therefore do them still. But we cannot honor God more than by looking, from former experience, for great things from the great God.

“We have waited for Him; He will save us; we have waited for Him, and we will rejoice in His salvation.” What a child of God gives thanks for, and rejoices in, and labors for, is more and more experience of his salvation. “We will rejoice in His salvation.” There is not a stronger word in all the Scripture, nor in nature. He does not speak of rejoicing in this or that benefit, but in his salvation, that is, in deliverance from all evil. We will rejoice in his preservation; when he has delivered us, we will rejoice in his advancement of us, and we will rejoice in his salvation. And therefore, when the wisdom of heaven would include all in one word, he uses the word Jesus, all happiness in that word, that pregnant, full word, a Savior.

So God’s dealing with his children is salvation. He is the God of salvation, or a saving God. And God sent his name from heaven, and the angels brought it, the name of Jesus. Therefore look to the full sense of it. We have a Savior who will answer to his name; as he is Jesus, so he will save his people from their sins, Matt 1:21. And therefore we will rejoice in his salvation. God dealt with us like a God, when he delivered us from all misery, from all sins, and advanced us to all the happiness that nature is capable of. As he said before, he will wipe away all tears from all faces, and take away the rebukes of all people. He will punish the wicked with eternal destruction. And if he advances a people, he will be salvation to them, and he can say no more than that.

And this shows that the children of God rejoice, more than in anything else, in salvation, because it is the salvation of God, and because God is salvation itself. Heaven would not be heaven if Jesus, and God in our nature, were not there. And therefore the apostle says, “I desire to depart,” not to be dissolved, “and be with Christ, which is far better.” The sight of God, especially in our nature, God the second person taking our nature so that we might be happy, will make us happy forever. Loving God, and joying in God, and enjoying God make full happiness; but that is not the cause of joy in heaven: the cause of all is God’s influence flowing into us. Here in the world our happiness comes through means: in God’s revealing himself to us by his Holy Spirit, in the use of means, in his dealings and deliverances, letting us see him by his grace, so that we see him, and joy and delight in him forever. It is no good love that rests in any of God’s blessings for their own sake. It is a harlot’s affection to love the gift more than the giver. So the saints of God all desire to see him as they may, and to joy in God, and enjoy God himself, and to see God in our nature, and to be with him forever. Before, he spoke of a feast; and if the feast-maker is not there, what is it all? At a funeral feast there is plenty of food, but the feast-maker is gone. In heaven there is joy; but where is God, where is Christ, he who has done so much and suffered so much for us, who has taken possession of heaven and keeps a place for us there? What is heaven without him? Salvation, severed from him, is nothing.

We will say when we are there, Behold, here is David, Abraham, St. John; here are the martyrs! Yes, but here is Christ, here is God, here is our Savior, the cause of all; and the seeing of him in them, that he will be glorious in his saints, is what makes us rejoice. We will see all our friends in heaven. There we will see the excellence of the happiness of Christ, his love, his grace, his mercy.

The words are spoken with a kind of glorying: “Behold, this is our God.” So the joy of a Christian ends in glory, and in the highest degree of glory, as you#super[+] have it in Rom 5:3, “We also glory in our sufferings; we glory in the hope of glory”; indeed, we glory in God as ours, reconciled. And if we glory in him now as a God reconciled, what will we do in heaven? Can a worldling glory in his riches, his greatness, his favor with such a man, as Haman did? And will not a Christian glory in his God, and make his boast in his God? And therefore in this world we should learn to glory, before we come to that glory in heaven, especially when we are set upon by anything that is apt to discourage us. Glory then in our Head. Perhaps a Christian has no wealth, no great income to glory in; yes, but he has a God to glory in; let him glory in him. The world may take everything else from him, but not his God. So it is with the church in the Song of Solomon 5. The virgins put the church to describe her beloved: “How is your beloved better than another? My beloved is dazzling and ruddy, outstanding among ten thousand.” Then she goes on in particulars, “my beloved is thus and thus”; and if you#super[+] would know what my beloved is, “this is my beloved.” So a Christian who has a spirit of faith should glory in God here, for heaven is begun here; and he should glory in Christ his Savior, and should set Christ against all discouragements and oppositions. If you#super[+] want to know what my beloved is, “this is my beloved, outstanding among ten thousand.” Ps 115:3. “Our God is in heaven; He does as He pleases, in heaven and on earth and in the depths.” Indeed, we make our boast of God, says the psalmist, when there is occasion. “This is the #smallcaps[Lord], this is our God; we have waited for Him,” especially in times of affliction; and what is the reason? This will hold out to eternity. “This is our God.” As in Revelation, it is a plea and a glory forever; for God is our happiness. As the schoolmen say, he is our objective happiness and our formal happiness; he is our happiness, as he is ours, and he is ours in life and death, and forever. So there is always ground for glorying; only God reveals himself to be ours little by little, as we are able to bear him. He is ours in our worst times. “My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me?” Yet my God still, Matt 27:46.

He is our God to death, and he is ours in heaven. “This is our God; we will rejoice in Him.” And therefore we may well boast of God, because in God is everlasting salvation. If we boasted in anything else, our boasting would end with the thing itself; but if we rejoice in God, we rejoice in what lasts as long as our souls, and goes along with the soul to all eternity.

And therefore we should learn to rejoice in God, and then we will never be ashamed. It is spoken here with a kind of exulting, a kind of triumphing over all oppositions: “Behold, this is our God.” Beloved, this, that God is our God, and Christ is ours, is the ground of rejoicing, and of all happiness. All joy, all comfort is founded upon this, our share in God; and therefore,

+ We must make this good while we live here, that God is our God; and so that we may do so, observe this. Christ is called Emmanuel, God with us. God, in the second person, is God-man, and so God with us; and the Father in Emmanuel is God with us too. So we are God the Father’s, because we are Christ’s. “All things are yours#super[+],” says the apostle, “whether Paul or Apollos … the present or the future.” Why? “Because you#super[+] are Christ’s” (1 Cor 3:22, 23). Yes, but what if I am Christ’s? Christ is God’s. So we must be Christ’s, and then we will be God’s. If Christ is ours, God is ours, for God is Emmanuel; in Christ, Emmanuel, God is with us in Christ, who is with us. God is reconciled to us in him who is God and man, in our nature. And therefore get into Christ by faith, and get union, and get communion; by prayer open our souls to him, welcome his speeches to us by his word and Spirit and blessed promptings, and open our spirits to him, and so maintain a blessed fellowship.
+ Make it good that God is our God by daily acquaintance. These speeches at the latter end are founded on acquaintance before. “This is our God.” Grace and glory are knit together indissolubly. If God is our God here, he will be ours also in glory; if not here, not in glory. There is a communion with God here before communion with him in glory; and therefore make it good that God is our God here first, by union with him. And then maintain daily acquaintance with him, by seeing him with the eye of faith, by speaking to him, and hearing him speak to us by his Spirit, joining in his ordinances; and then he will acknowledge us, and be acquainted with us. In heaven we will say, “Behold, this is our God.” We have had sweet acquaintance with one another: he by his Spirit with me, and I by my prayers with him. Our Savior Christ will not be without us in heaven. We are part of his mystical body, and heaven would not be heaven to Christ without us. With reverence be it spoken, we are the fullness of Christ, as he is the fullness of his church. And if he should lack us, in some sort he would be miserable, having fixed upon us as the objects of his eternal love. What case would he be in if he should lose that object? And therefore, as we glory in him, he glories in us. “Who is this coming up from the wilderness?” Who? “His beloved” (Song 3:6). And, “Woman, is this your faith?” (Matt 15:28). He wonders at the graces of the church, as the church wonders at him. “This is the #smallcaps[Lord].” The church cannot be without him, nor he without the church. These words are spoken with a kind of wonder: “Behold, this is the #smallcaps[Lord]; we will rejoice in Him.” So I say, as there is thanks and joy, so there is wonder: “Behold! See!” This is a God worth beholding; and so he wonders at the graces of his children. Beloved, there is nothing in the world worth wondering at. Sapientis non est admirari. It was a saying of the proud philosopher: a wise man will not wonder at anything, for he knows the cause. But in heaven our faculties are lifted up so high that there is nothing but matter for wonder, things “no eye has seen, no ear has heard, no heart has imagined” (1 Cor 2:9). They are things beyond expression, and nothing fits them but wonder at the great things granted to the church.

And as with wonder, so with invitation. That is the nature of true thankfulness. There is no envy in spiritual things. No man envies another the light of the Scriptures; but, behold! See, with wonder and with invitation to all others, “This is the #smallcaps[Lord].”

Let us therefore rejoice beforehand at the glorious times to come, both for ourselves and for others; be stirring up and urging one another to glory, and rejoice in God our salvation.

+ And, therefore, let us all learn to be stirred up by this not to be offended with Christ, or with religion. Do not be offended, says Augustine, with the littleness of religion. Everything in religion is little to the eyes of the world. A Christian is a despised person, and the church the lowliest part of the world, in regard to outward glory. But,
+ Consider, along with the littleness, and lowliness, and despisedness of the church, the glory to come. The time will come when we will rejoice, and not only see, but boast with wonder, to the stirring up of others, “Behold, this is the #smallcaps[Lord].” And, therefore, say with our Savior Christ, “Blessed is he who is not offended because of Me” (Matt 11:6), nor with religion. There is a time coming that will make amends for all. Who in the world can say at the hour of death and the day of judgment, Behold, this is my wealth, these are my honors! Alas! the greatest persons must stand naked to give account; all must stand on even ground to hold up their hands at the great bar. We may say to the carnal, presumptuous man, Behold, this is the man who put his confidence in his riches. And none but reconciled Christians can say, “Behold, this is our God.” Therefore take heed of being offended with anything in religion.
+ Again, if the time to come is so surpassingly glorious, let us not be afraid to die, let us not be overmuch cast down, for it will end in glory. And let us always be in expectation of good times, wait for this blessed time to come, and never be content with any condition so as to set up our rest here. We may write upon everything, Hic non est requies vestra [Here is not your rest]. Our rest is still to come; these things are passing. And therefore rest content with nothing here. Heaven is our center, our element, our happiness; and everything is contentedly happy, and thrives, in its element. The birds in the air, the fish in the sea, the beasts on the earth rest there as in their center. And what is our place forever is heaven, is God. The immediate enjoying of God in heaven is our rest, our element, and we will never rest until we are there. And therefore he who sets up his rest here is called a fool for it in the gospel. Whoever says, I have enough, and will now take contentment in my goods, he is a fool. “There remains a Sabbath rest for the people of God” (Heb 4:9), but it is not here.
+ Nor rest in any measure of grace or comfort. What is faith to sight? We have hope, an anchor and a helmet, which keeps up many a soul, as the cork keeps it from sinking. What is this hope to the enjoyment of what we hope for? Here we have love, many love tokens from God. Yes, but what is love to union? Ours is only a love of desire. We are only in motion here; we lie in motion only, and our desires are not fulfilled. What is this love to the fulfilling of the union with the thing beloved forever? Here we have the communion of saints. But what is this communion of saints to communion with God forever? We have infirmities here, as others do, which breed jealousies and suspicion. Yes, but we will have communion in heaven, and there will be nothing in us to displease others, but everlasting friendship. Indeed, our communion will be with perfect souls. Our communion of saints here is our heaven upon earth, but it is communion with imperfect souls. Peace we have; yes, but it is mixed peace, peace in the midst of enemies. There we will have peace without enemies. Christ now rules in the midst of enemies. In heaven he will rule in the midst of his friends. So we can imagine no condition here, however good, that is not imperfect. And therefore rest in nothing in the world, no, not in any measure of grace, any measure of comfort, until we are in heaven; but wait for the time to come, “and rejoice in hope, by which we are saved” (Rom 12:12). Wait continually; and though we do not have contentment here, yet this is not our home; this is a good refreshment on the way. As when the children of Israel came from Babylon, they had wells on the way: as in the Valley of Baca, Ps 84:6, they dug wells. So from Babylon to Jerusalem we have many sweet refreshments; but they are refreshments far from home, on the way. God digs many wells; we have breasts of consolation to comfort us; yes, but they are only for the way. And therefore let us answer all temptations, and not take contentment in anything here. It is good, but it is not our home. Cui dulcis peregrinatio, non amat patriam [He to whom the pilgrimage is sweet does not love his homeland]. If we have eternity, if we love heaven, we cannot be overmuch taken with anything on the way.
+ And so for the church, let us not be overmuch dejected at the desolation of the church, but pray for a spirit of faith, which makes things real to the soul, and presents them to the soul as present, sees Babylon fallen, presents things in the Scripture’s way of speaking, in the words, “Babylon is fallen,” since all the enemies of the church fall. Mighty is the Lord who has spoken, and he will perform it; and, as the angel says, “It is done” (Rev 16:17).

So the time will come before long when it will be said, “It is done.” The church will be gathered, and then, “Behold, this is our God.”

It was the comfort of the believing Jews that the Gentiles would come in. And why should it not be the comfort of the Gentiles that there are blessed times for the ancient people of God, when they will all cry out and say, “Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him long, and He will save us”? Therefore do not be overmuch discouraged at whatever present desolation the church lies under. If it were not for this, “we would be of all men most miserable,” as Paul says, 1 Cor 15:19. But there are times to come when we will rejoice, and rejoice forever, and make our boast of the Lord. If it were not so, “we would be of all men most miserable.” Although our happiness is still to come, yet of all persons he is most happy who has Christ and heaven. The very foretaste of happiness is worth all the world. The inward peace of conscience, joy in the Holy Spirit, the beginnings of the image of God and of happiness here, are worth all the enjoyments of the world. Ask any Christian whether he would change places with the greatest worldling and be in his condition; he would not exchange his place in grace for all the worldling’s glory. And therefore, set heaven aside, the very firstfruits are better than all the harvest of the world. Let us therefore get the soul raised by faith to see its happiness. We need it all, for until the soul gets a frame raised up to see its happiness here, especially in the world to come, it is not in a frame fit for any service, or that will not stoop to any base sin. Where the affections are so possessed, they look upon all base courses as unworthy of their hope. What! I, who hope to rejoice forever with God in heaven, who am an heir of heaven, who have the image of God upon me, who am in covenant with God, to take any brutish course, to place my happiness in things lower than myself, I who have God to delight in, a God in covenant, who has taken me into covenant with himself! So I say, in all solicitations to sin, get ourselves into a frame that may stand firm and immovable.

In all troubles let us know that we have a God in covenant, so that we may joy in him here, and rejoice with him in heaven forever hereafter.
